@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · MIRIAM · STANDING" m="TKC: TL · FR 0 · FR 6–9 · FR 15–17; VOL. I · {v1:arrangement}"
# The clerk's version and the true one
note: Abandonment and coerced reassignment.
rec:
On the ledger | Commonwife registration quietly deactivated in 2090, no forwarding Dominion logged (TL). Within four years of her Binding she was "unrecognizable to her own mother" (TL).
In 2084 | Thomas left voluntarily, and she was reassigned to another man's household (FR 0, FR 3). OPEN QUESTION: Who decided it, and what was her status while it happened (Vol. I, {v1:arrangement})?
What she protected them from | The household she'd been reassigned to, and the instability of dragging four children on a search. She hadn't found her father yet, so she couldn't have named him as the threat (FR 9).
Unreachable | Being unreachable wasn't selective. No version existed where she was hidden from her father and available to her children (FR 15).
Her father's house | He'd been Crownward-resident and close to the Vanes the whole time, and surviving an unregistered ceremony wasn't an oversight. He was useful to somebody, and her children would be too (FR 17).
prompt: What her days were like in the reassigned household, and the moment she decided to go | fill

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · MIRIAM · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 1 OF 2" m="TKC: TL · DOM · FR 8 · FR 18; VOL. I · {v1:teaches} · {v1:lived} · {v1:arrangement}"
# What she believes
rec:
Who taught her | Celeste, who refused every term the Empire offered and raised her to do the same (TL). The Salt Ceremony the night before her Binding: what to hold, what to hide, what not to hand over (DOM).
What she believed at twenty | She loved Thomas, and she did (TL). INFERENCE: That love could be a private thing inside a public registration.
What she gave away | *Selah*, from the Psalms, to her first daughter (TL). INFERENCE: She knew the Psalms well enough to choose from them.
What she believes now | OPEN QUESTION: The files give her conviction and no expectation of being believed when she returns (FR 18), and nothing about God.
empire: What the Temple teaches about a woman like her
A Commonwife is expected to bear children on the Covenant timeline (MEN), and an abandoned one can be moved to another household (FR 0). POSSIBLE: The Temple cites Psalm 68.6, "God setteth the solitary in families," as the warrant for reassignment, and stops before the rest of the verse, "he bringeth out those which are bound with chains." For a mother who leaves, it has Titus 2.4–5, "to love their children… keepers at home."
/end
prompt: What Miriam would say to God if she believed he was listening, and what she'd say if she didn't | fill

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · MIRIAM · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 2 OF 2" m="EXOD 2.1–10; EXOD 15.20–21; NUM 12; NUM 20.1–2; MIC 6.4; RUTH 1.20–21; PS 68.6; ISA 49.15"
# The text
rec:
Her name | Miriam the prophet, the first woman the Torah calls one (Exod 15.20). Mariam in Greek, so every Mary in the New Testament (Vol. I, {v1:miriam}).
Miriam at the river · Exodus 2 | POSSIBLE: If the unnamed sister at the river is Miriam, as tradition holds, she watches from a distance as her brother is placed in the reeds, then arranges for their own mother to nurse him. **Where it breaks:** The biblical Miriam stays near the child she placed. This one placed hers and went out of reach.
Numbers 12 | POSSIBLE: Struck and put outside the camp for seven days, and the camp doesn't move until she's brought back. **Where it breaks:** Whether anyone in the Empire waits for this one is the question (Vol. I, {v1:miriam}).
Naomi · Ruth 1.20–21 | POSSIBLE: "I went out full, and the LORD hath brought me home again empty." Call me Mara. **Where it breaks:** Naomi comes home to people who recognize her. Miriam comes home to four children who've each kept a different version of the day she left (FR 11).
tab:
| She'd quote | Never read to her |
| Exodus 15.21, "Sing ye to the LORD," the song her name carries | Micah 6.4, where God names Miriam with Moses and Aaron as the three he sent |
| Ruth 1.21, coming home empty | Numbers 20.1–2, her death and the next line, "there was no water" |
| _ | Isaiah 49.15, "Can a woman forget her sucking child… yet will I not forget thee" |
box: Volume One
Miriam, 375. Numbers, Miriam and Aaron, 152. The sea, the song and Miriam, 282. Exodus, six women, 272. Ruth and Naomi, {v1:ruth} and 109. Commonwives and reassignment, 512.
/end

