@essay rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · MIRIAM · THE MACHINERY AROUND HER"
# The machinery around her
drop: The Empire never had to remove Miriam. It only had to file her. A Commonwife is a registration status (FR 1), and a status can be moved. When her husband left, the system that had approved her Binding reassigned her to another man's household (FR 0, FR 3).
There's also the registration itself, deactivated without a forwarding Dominion (TL), so on paper she simply stops. Her father's household sits in the Crownward, close to the Vanes, where a man who survived an unregistered ceremony is useful to somebody (FR 17). Someone stopped her one letter (FR 16). And her return is triggered by something she doesn't control: the arrangement that kept her unreachable ended (FR 18).
INTERPRETATION: Every piece of her story is somebody else's paperwork, which is why her return has to be the first thing she does on her own authority.
side:
chips: FR 0, FR 15–18
k: Book two
stext: Her father is the only person watching the Trial who understands what he's looking at (FR 18).
marg
see: Vol. I, Commonwives and reassignment, 512.
/end

